---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-16"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Charging · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>AGM Battery Charging: Voltages, Limits and What Overcharge Does</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        AGM is sealed, and the seal changes the charging rules. The number-one AGM killer is not the chemistry — it
        is charging it like a flooded battery.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> AGM is charged with a <strong>voltage-limited profile</strong> — absorption
        around <strong>14.4–14.7V</strong> for a 12V battery, then float — and it must <strong>not receive the
        equalisation charge</strong> used on flooded batteries. Overcharging drives the valve to vent electrolyte
        that <strong>cannot be replaced</strong>, drying the glass mat and shortening life sharply.
      </div>

      <h2>AGM vs flooded charging rules</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Flooded</th><th>AGM</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Absorption (12V)</strong></td><td>~14.4–14.8V</td><td>~14.4–14.7V (stay in limit)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Equalisation</strong></td><td>Yes, periodically</td><td><strong>No</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Overcharge result</strong></td><td>Water loss — refillable</td><td>Vented electrolyte — permanent</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why overcharge is permanent damage</h2>
      <p>
        In a flooded battery, overcharge gasses off water that you top up. In AGM, the
        <a href="https://technology.worldbatteryhub.com/vrla/">VRLA</a> recombination normally recycles the oxygen —
        but if the charge voltage exceeds the recombination rate, pressure builds until the <strong>valve
        vents</strong>, and the lost electrolyte is gone forever in the sealed design. Each overcharge event
        permanently dries the mat.
      </p>

      <h2>The three rules</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Use an AGM setting</strong> — never charge on a flooded or equalisation profile.</li>
        <li><strong>Respect the voltage limit</strong> — keep absorption inside the manufacturer's stated range.</li>
        <li><strong>Temperature-compensate</strong> — hot batteries need lower voltage; the charger should adjust (see <a href="/lead-acid/lead-acid-charging/">lead-acid charging</a>).</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>The voltage numbers</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>12V system</th><th>Absorption limit</th><th>Float</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Flooded</td><td>~14.4–14.8V</td><td>~13.2–13.5V</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AGM</td><td>~14.4–14.7V</td><td>~13.5–13.8V</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gel</td><td>~14.1–14.4V</td><td>~13.5–13.8V</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        Typical manufacturer ranges — always follow the specific battery's datasheet; the charger must match the
        type, or the battery is slowly damaged every cycle.
      </p>


      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>I would argue:</strong> The AGM battery's worst enemy is a charger set for the wrong battery — and the
          single highest-value maintenance habit is simply using the AGM setting, which costs nothing and prevents
          the one failure AGM cannot recover from.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why:</strong> AGM's sealed advantage carries a sealed penalty: what vents can never be refilled.
          The charging discipline — right profile, right limit, no equalisation — is therefore not a refinement but the
          entire game for AGM lifespan.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What voltage should an AGM battery be charged at?</h3>
      <p>AGM is charged with a voltage-limited profile — roughly 14.4-14.7V absorption for a 12V battery — and must not receive the equalisation charge used on flooded batteries. The charger must have an AGM setting.</p>
      <h3>What happens if you overcharge an AGM battery?</h3>
      <p>Overcharge drives the recombination reaction too hard, building pressure until the valve vents — permanently losing electrolyte that cannot be replaced in the sealed design. Repeated overcharge dries the mat and shortens life sharply.</p>
<h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Does the charger have a dedicated AGM profile with the correct absorption and float voltages?</li>
  <li>Is the charger's maximum voltage within AGM's tight limits, since overcharge is permanent damage?</li>
  <li>Is temperature compensation applied, or will the battery be charged at the wrong voltage in heat or cold?</li>
  <li>Will the charger avoid equalization or desulfation modes that are wrong for AGM?</li>
</ul>
<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>AGM charging is a tight-voltage discipline — the correct profile, no overcharge and no equalization. Overcharge is permanent, not recoverable, so the charger profile is the difference between a long-lived AGM and an early failure.</p>

<h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Cross-verified from battery engineering references and manufacturer charging documentation.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Manufacturer charging documentation (Varta, Bosch, Exide) — AGM voltage limits.</li>
        <li>Battery engineering references — VRLA recombination and venting.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
